  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1126 AM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe upper-level ridge holds in place with hot temperatures\nexpected through Saturday. Lingering monsoonal moisture riding\nalong the ridge will be just enough to have a daily chance for\nafternoon showers and thunderstorms today and tomorrow. Wildfire \nsmoke from the PacNW will continue to funnel into the region \nproducing hazy skies and, at times, some degradation in air \nquality across the region.\n\nTowards the weekend the ridge breaks down as a strong trough moves\ninto the region. This will bring a short but significant cool down\nto the region Sunday through Tuesday but will also work to bring\nstrong winds that will last Saturday evening into Sunday evening.\nThis combination of hot, dry, and windy conditions will lead to\nincreased fire weather concerns. -thor\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms Today:\n\nScattered thunderstorms are expected Wednesday and Thursday\nafternoon across Southwest and portions of Central Montana. While\nsevere weather is generally not expected there is still a concern\nfor frequent lightning and strong, gusty winds.\n\n\nHeat This Weekend:\n\nThere is good confidence in impactful heat settling in Saturday. \nThe European Model Extreme Forecast Index shows a 0.7 to 0.95 east\nof the Continental Divide Saturday, meaning it will be unusually \nhot for this time of year. There remains some model and run to run\ninconsistency with how hot it will get. Another big question mark\nis the wildfire smoke and if an increase fire weather behavior \nacross the Pacific Northwest will produce more smoke and dampen \nthe high temperatures. Regardless, there remains good confidence \nin impactful heat Saturday, especially across North-central MT.\n\n\nWinds This Weekend:\n\nModels still hint at the increasing potential for gusty to strong\nwinds develop along the Rocky Mountain Front and North-Central MT\nlate Saturday into Sunday. Models have been consistently showing \na 50-70kt 700mb jet developing along the Rocky Mountain Front and\nNorth-Central MT plains late Saturday night into mid Sunday \nmorning. The timing of this jet is not the most ideal for high \nwinds, but good ridgetop stability suggests mountain wave activity\nwill be possible. Another concern is impact on recreation along \nthe Rocky Mountain Front/back country due to falling trees. Right \nnow, there's a 40-60% chance for 60 mph winds along the immediate\nNorthern Rocky Mountain Front Foothills. One important\nconsideration for this weekend will be the wind climatology for\nthe region with may locations along the RMF potentially exceeding\nthe 90th percentile for this time of the year. While the \nlingering discrepancies in the models make things too uncertain to\nissue a high wind watch at this time, it is something that will be\nconsidered with future forecasts as more model data becomes\navailable.\n\nAdditional concerns for the weekend are related to elevated to\ncritical fire weather conditions Sunday afternoon through Sunday\nevening (see the FIRE WEATHER section for more detail). \n-Wilson/thor",
